     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi All,</div><div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n    <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/posts/76454291/timeline\" aria-label=\"Timeline\" aria-describedby=\"--stacks-s-tooltip-dlpgjboi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stackoverflow.com/posts/76454291/timeline&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243500248000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3mrx7CtF2Pe8sK5zwVSruI\"></a><div><div>\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        \r\n\r\n<div>\r\n    \r\n    <div>\r\n                \r\n<p>I&#39;m uncertain how to establish the difference, if any, of <strong>two linear models each representing a different correlation</strong>. Consider, for example, this toy data, where <code>z</code> is the grouping variable:</p>\r\n<pre><code>df <span>&lt;-</span> data.frame<span>(</span>\r\n  x <span>=</span> <span>c</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>:</span><span>10</span><span>,</span> <span>1</span><span>:</span><span>10</span><span>)</span><span>,</span>\r\n  y <span>=</span> <span>c</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span>4</span><span>,</span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span>4</span><span>,</span><span>6</span><span>,</span><span>8</span><span>,</span>\r\n        <span>1</span><span>,</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span>4</span><span>,</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span>5</span><span>)</span><span>,</span>\r\n  z <span>=</span> <span>c</span><span>(</span><span>rep</span><span>(</span><span>&quot;A&quot;</span><span>,</span><span>10</span><span>)</span><span>,</span> <span>rep</span><span>(</span><span>&quot;B&quot;</span><span>,</span><span>10</span><span>)</span><span>)</span>\r\n<span>)</span>\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p>The two models in questions are:</p>\r\n<pre><code>lm1 <span>&lt;-</span> lm<span>(</span>df<span>$</span>y<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z<span>==</span><span>&quot;A&quot;</span><span>]</span> <span>~</span> df<span>$</span>x<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z<span>==</span><span>&quot;A&quot;</span><span>]</span><span>)</span>\r\nlm2 <span>&lt;-</span> lm<span>(</span>df<span>$</span>y<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z<span>==</span><span>&quot;B&quot;</span><span>]</span> <span>~</span> df<span>$</span>x<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z<span>==</span><span>&quot;B&quot;</span><span>]</span><span>)</span>\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p>I seem to know that <code>anova</code> can be used to determine whether <strong>two models representing the same data</strong> are different:</p>\r\n<pre><code>anova<span>(</span>lm1<span>,</span> lm2<span>)</span>\r\nAnalysis of Variance Table\r\n\r\nResponse<span>:</span> df<span>$</span>y<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z <span>==</span> <span>&quot;A&quot;</span><span>]</span>\r\n                  Df Sum Sq Mean Sq <span>F</span> value   Pr<span>(</span><span>&gt;</span><span>F</span><span>)</span>   \r\ndf<span>$</span>x<span>[</span>df<span>$</span>z <span>==</span> <span>&quot;A&quot;</span><span>]</span>  <span>1</span> <span>24.546</span> <span>24.5455</span>  <span>13.043</span> <span>0.006866</span> <span>*</span><span>*</span>\r\nResiduals          <span>8</span> <span>15.055</span>  <span>1.8818</span>                    \r\n<span>-</span><span>-</span><span>-</span>\r\nSignif. codes<span>:</span>  <span>0</span> ‘<span>*</span><span>*</span><span>*</span>’ <span>0.001</span> ‘<span>*</span><span>*</span>’ <span>0.01</span> ‘<span>*</span>’ <span>0.05</span> ‘.’ <span>0.1</span> ‘ ’ <span>1</span>\r\nWarning message<span>:</span>\r\nIn anova.lmlist<span>(</span>object<span>,</span> ...<span>)</span> <span>:</span>\r\n  models with response ‘<span>&quot;df$y[df$z == \\&quot;B\\&quot;]&quot;</span>’ removed because response differs from model <span>1</span>\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p>But can <code>anova</code> be used to see if two correlations of essentially <strong>different data</strong> are significantly different? Are there alternative and better ways?</p><p><br></p><p>Many thanks in advance</p><p>Christoph<br></p>\r\n    </div></div></div><br><span class=\"gmail_signature_prefix\">-- </span><br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg<br></div><div>Projekt-Leiter DFG-Forschungsprojekt &quot;Multimodale Turn-Abschlusssignale&quot;</div><div dir=\"ltr\"><a href=\"https://gepris.dfg.de/gepris/projekt/497779797\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://gepris.dfg.de/gepris/projekt/497779797&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243500248000&amp;usg=AOvVaw38owr3ApmHQnOnQz2uK_Fd\">(https://gepris.dfg.de/gepris/projekt/497779797</a>) <br></div><div dir=\"ltr\">Homepage:<font color=\"#888888\"><font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"grc\"> <a href=\"http://paul.igl.uni-freiburg.de/ruehlemann/?Home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://paul.igl.uni-freiburg.de/ruehlemann/?Home&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793243500248000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3xd-PUTY2SjBVqGGsjwznb\">http://paul.igl.uni-freiburg.de/ruehlemann/?Home</a><br></span></font></font></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><font color=\"#888888\"><font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"grc\"><br></span></font></font><font size=\"2\"><span lang=\"grc\"><s